views {

    // 'index' is reserved: LikeC4 generates its own landing view if no view claims
    // that id, so this one keeps it and carries its level in the title alone.
    view index {
        title '[C1] MicroWorld - System Context'
        description 'Why MicroWorld exists. One application reaching three hardware targets is the point of the product, and the fan-out states it by shape rather than by adjective. Devices exchange messages, not bytes, so the developer never writes link-level code. The UE5 familiarity claim is a property of the API, so it lives in the product description rather than pretending to be a relationship.'
        include developer
        include deviceFirmware
        include microworld
        include esp32Device
        include picoDevice
        include developmentPc
        autoLayout TopBottom
    }

    view c2Systems {
        title '[C2] MicroWorld'
        description 'Six systems, each named for the responsibility it owns. Networking is the session boundary above Messaging: it owns Client/Server roles, peer admission, liveness and logical routing, while Messaging owns links, routes, channels, serialization and reliable delivery. Engine owns optional Messaging and Networking and gives each World a non-owning Network pointer. Networking never sees a device or endpoint; Transport never sees Networking, Messaging or Engine. Messaging and Transport meet only at the Core transport-device interface, and the application composition root registers concrete devices and advances each exactly once. Direct Messaging and direct Transport remain independently usable.'
        include microworld
        include microworld.core
        include espIdf
        include hostOs
        include microworld.engine
        include microworld.messagingSystem
        include microworld.networking
        include microworld.transport
        include microworld.app
        // The transport device interface is a Core child shown at C3: rolled up to
        // this level its realised-by edges read as Core depending on Transport.
        exclude microworld.core.transportDeviceInterface -> *
        exclude * -> microworld.core.transportDeviceInterface
        autoLayout TopBottom
    }

    view c3Engine of microworld.engine {
        title '[C3] Engine'
        description 'C3, because three boundaries are easy to get wrong. The application drives only the Engine Runtime Interface, so world, storage, messaging and typed creation stay concrete. The deferred spawn barrier constructs actors only at safe points, and generation-checked handles detect stale identities. The identity-versus-lifetime split is deliberately not repeated on these eight children - it is a C2 fact and the C2 subtitle carries it.'
        include *
        autoLayout TopBottom
    }

    view c3Messaging of microworld.messagingSystem {
        title '[C3] Messaging System'
        description 'C3, because four things here are decisions a contributor would otherwise guess wrong. A channel is the unit of composition - one name, one reliability declaration, and optionally one device with the address it sends to - and a channel without a device is not degraded, it is the local mode: delivery to this node always happens, a device only adds remote reach. Reliable delivery lives here and not below, because Transport answers acceptance and never delivery - an acknowledgement is a message from the peer, so the resend loop must sit where messages are understood. Core contributes two contracts: the transport device interface is the only dependency on bytes, so a channel names no medium and no medium names a message; FMessagingSystem realises IPlaySystem, so its owner drives lifecycle and frame-advance turns through the Core contract. There is no router box because routing is the system itself - a message goes to the channel it was sent on, and each subscription filters by message name or takes everything. FMessagingSystem is the one object the engine creates and hands out - everything else is reached through it.'
        include *
        include microworld.core.playSystemInterface
        include microworld.core.transportDeviceInterface
        autoLayout TopBottom
    }

    view c3Networking of microworld.networking {
        title '[C3] Networking'
        description 'Networking owns one bounded logical-session policy above Messaging. It has only Client and Server roles, validates a route together with a generation-checked peer id, and exposes peer events without routes. It uses two private Messaging wire channels; accepted routed application messages are republished locally with opaque source metadata. It never accesses or advances a device, and it neither adds authentication nor exposes a public connection object.'
        include *
        include microworld.messagingSystem.system
        autoLayout TopBottom
    }

    view c3Transport of microworld.transport {
        title '[C3] Transport'
        description 'Flat on purpose. The shape every medium realises - send one packet to an address, drain arrivals, cap the payload - is declared in Core, which is why the declaration sits at the top of this view without belonging to this system: a messaging channel sends through it, a medium realises it, and neither names the other. Configuration is deliberately not part of that shape - an SSID and a baud rate have nothing in common - so an application names a concrete device type to configure it and needs nothing concrete afterwards. Uniform is the shape and not the meaning - each medium reads the address its own way, and a point-to-point one ignores it. Receiving does not make bytes appear either: a device is also a play system, and whoever owns it installs it. Three things are then deliberately absent. Send never blocks, so it answers that it had no room rather than waiting. Nothing here sequences, acknowledges or retries, because Send answers acceptance and never delivery. And nothing here splits: a device publishes the largest payload it can take and refuses anything longer. All three sit above this level in the Messaging System, and keeping them out is what keeps a device a device. The frame codec has exactly two edges, and the two missing ones are the point of the view - Wi-Fi and Bluetooth deliver bounded datagrams, while radio and wired media receive a continuous stream and must recover message boundaries. Each device is one medium, not one class: the three wired buses share a box because nothing they differ in - wiring, who clocks the line, how a peer is named - changes the shape they realise. Below the media sit the platform implementations, drawn outside the product boundary because we do not own them: a medium here wraps somebody elses radio or bus driver, it does not invent one. Read the arrows into them and you get which board can do what - ESP-IDF realises every medium, a development host realises only Wi-Fi, and the RP2040 reaches only LoRa. LoRa is the one medium whose arrows read differently: it is not realised by a platform at all, it merely takes a byte stream from one, because the E32 protocol is ours and portable while the wire underneath is not. Bluetooth has no realisation yet and appears anyway, because the architecture decides which media exist and the code follows.'
        include *
        include microworld.core.transportDeviceInterface
        include espIdf
        include picoSdk
        include hostOs
        autoLayout TopBottom
    }

}
